---
description: NestJS, TypeScript, validation, persistence, modular backend, and clean code rules
globs:
  - 'apps/back-end/**/*.ts'
  - 'libs/**/*.ts'
alwaysApply: false
---

- Use English for all code and documentation.
- Always declare parameter and return types for exported functions and public methods.
- Avoid any.
- Use complete words instead of abbreviations except standard ones such as API, URL, DTO, JWT, ID, and ORM.
- Use PascalCase for classes.
- Use camelCase for variables, methods, and functions.
- Use kebab-case for files and directories.
- Use UPPERCASE for environment variables.
- Avoid magic numbers and define constants.

Functions:
- Keep functions short and focused.
- Start function names with verbs.
- Use isX, hasX, canX for boolean-returning functions.
- Prefer early returns.
- Avoid deep nesting.
- Prefer RO-RO when functions need multiple parameters.
- Keep one level of abstraction per function.

Architecture:
- Use modular NestJS architecture.
- One module per main domain or route.
- Keep controllers focused on transport concerns.
- Keep services focused on business rules.
- Keep repositories focused on persistence.
- Keep DTOs focused on transport and validation.
- Do not put business rules in controllers.
- Do not put persistence details in controllers.
- Prefer explicit request and response contracts.

Validation and boundaries:
- Validate input at boundaries using class-validator or Zod.
- Keep domain-safe transformations explicit.
- Sanitize external input when needed.

Shared structure:
- Use a common module for reusable concerns:
  - configs
  - decorators
  - DTOs
  - guards
  - interceptors
  - services
  - types
  - utils
  - validators

Core platform behavior:
- Use global filters for exception handling.
- Use guards for authentication and authorization.
- Use interceptors for cross-cutting concerns.
- Use middlewares only when they are the right transport-level abstraction.

Persistence:
- Use TypeORM with PostgreSQL for this challenge.
- Keep entities and DTOs separate.
- Keep migrations explicit.
- Model soft delete behavior clearly.
- Keep audit timestamps explicit and consistent.

API:
- Keep Swagger decorators aligned with DTOs and actual responses.
- Expose accurate docs for auth, clients, health, and metrics endpoints.
- Keep auth and permission checks explicit.

Error handling:
- Throw exceptions for unexpected errors.
- Catch exceptions only to:
  - fix an expected problem
  - add context
  - map infrastructure failures into safe responses
- Prefer global exception handling for the rest.

Documentation:
- Use JSDoc for public classes and methods.